//An even loopier zip file

.p|Circa 2005, Erling Ellingsen created the first <(https://alf.nu/ZipQuine)[zip
quine]>, that is to say a zip file which contains itself. In 2010 Russ Cox
created <(https://research.swtch.com/zip)[a gzip quine]> and documented the
entire process of creating it. In 2024 Ruben Van Mello created a
<(https://github.com/ruvmello/zip-quine-generator)[zip quine generator]> based
on Cox's description of zip quines, and created the first ever "loopy" zip
quine, that is to say a zip file `A` which contains another zip file `B` which
contains the original zip file `A`.

.p|In 2025 I began constructing zip bombs. I hand-crafted a twice-compressed
<(https://github.com/NateChoe1/zip-bomb/tree/main/primitive)[1 terabyte]> zlib
bomb, then wrote <(https://github.com/NateChoe1/ied)[a program]> to
automatically generate finite zip bombs with extremely high compression ratios,
then used some techniques from zip quines to build an
<(https://github.com/NateChoe1/zip-bomb/tree/main/quine)[infinitely large]> zip
bomb. Finally, last week I created my own
<(https://github.com/NateChoe1/heller)[zip quine generator]> which can handle
loopy zip quines of any order. For a sample, try extracting
<(https://github.com/NateChoe1/heller/raw/refs/heads/main/examples/z1.zip)[this
zip file]>.

.p|Zip, zlib, and gzip are all just wrappers around the
<(https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1951/)[Deflate]> compressed data format,
which uses LZ77 compression under the hood. I created a toy
<(https://natechoe1.github.io/lz77-puzzle/)[LZ77 model]>. The rest of this
article is going to use snippets from there.

#2|An intro to LZ77

.p|LZ77 compressed data is made up of two directives: `print` and `repeat`. The
`print` directive just prints out some number of literal bytes, or one line in
our toy model.

```"print 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|The second directive, `repeat`, takes in two arguments: a length and a
distance. `repeat` will look back `distance` lines of the /output/ and copy
`length` lines.

```"print 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "repeat 2 3

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "b
   "c

.p|Note that the length could be longer than the distance. In that case we just
copy over the newly created lines.

```"print 2
   "a
   "b
   "repeat 5 2

```"a
   "b
   "a
   "b
   "a
   "b
   "a

.p|Here's the actual Javascript code that implements the `repeat` block:

$</site/code.sh javascript
$|for (let j = 0; j < repLength; ++j) {
$|  output.push(output[output.length - offset]);
$|}

#2|Zip quines, headers, footers, and junk

.p|Can you create a quine in this model? That is to say, can you find some input
which decompresses into its own code? This is a really hard puzzle, I encourage
you to <(https://natechoe1.github.io/lz77-puzzle/)[try it for yourself]> before
I spoil the answer in the next paragraph.

.p|Here's a solution attributed to Russ Cox. I've labeled two especially
important lines:

```"print 0
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4     [1]
   "print 4
   "a              [2]
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|The reason why this puzzle is hard is that our input always seems to be
longer than our output. To illustrate this, let's try starting with a `print`
block.

```"print 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|The first line of our input must match the first line of our output, so line
`a` must be `print 4`:

```"print 4
   "print 4
   "b
   "c
   "d

```"print 4
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|Now the second lines of our input and output must match, so line `b` must
also be `print 4`:

```"print 4
   "print 4
   "print 4
   "c
   "d

```"print 4
   "print 4
   "c
   "d

.p|Then the third line has to match, and the fourth, and so on. It's very easy
to create an output that matches the beginning of our input, it's very
difficult to create an input that matches the beginning of our output. Line
`[1]` is the first point in our quine where the output overtakes the input.

.p|Coincidentally, our quine must end with a `print 4` block, where the actual
data that gets printed doesn't affect the final quine. I call these final four
lines "junk", because you can put any four lines in there while still
maintaining quine-ness. Also note that the first three lines of this quine could
be essentially anything, so long as they don't produce any output. In the toy
model, you can insert some arbitrary header and footer to your quines;
everything before the first `<<<<<\0` line and everything after the last
`>>>>>\0` line is completely ignored. In our model, this represents things like
gzip headers and footers, which may contain metadata about the file such as its
decompressed length or checksum.

```"a
   "b
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "a
   "b
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|Unfortunately, usually these headers and footers are longer than three lines
long. Can you find some way to insert an arbitrary length header or footer?

.p|Here's a quine with a longer header, the method used to produce this quine
can be generalized to a header of any length:

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "repeat 6 6
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 6 6
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|We start off with this sequence:

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "repeat 6 6

.p|which expands to this:

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6

.p|Now the input matches the output except that we have an extra `repeat 6 6`
line. We then append two `print 0` lines and treat that sequence as a length
three header.

.p|To get an arbitrary footer, we start by creating arbitrary junk:

```"print 0
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "e
   "f

.p|We start off with our initial quine, then use these four lines of junk:

```"[junk 4]
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6

.p|We then copy that junk and run its corresponding code:

```"[junk 4]
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "e
   "f

.p|The corresponding code then prints out 6 lines of junk. We can convert this
junk into an arbitrary length footer like this:

```"[junk 6]
   "repeat 6 6
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "repeat 6 6
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|In the junk we add a `repeat` line along with the footer we want, then we
repeat the footer from the junk to get the actual footer. Here's our final
quine, with an arbitrary header and footer:

```"a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "print 6
   "repeat 6 6
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 6 6
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 4
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "repeat 4 4
   "print 0
   "print 0
   "print 6
   "repeat 6 6
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "repeat 6 6
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|All of these solutions are built in to the toy model under the hidden
`COMPILE_MODE`.

```"COMPILE_MODE
   "compile_print 5
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   "quine 5 5
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "<<<<<
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d
   "compile_print 5
   ">>>>>
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

.p|The `quine` directive takes in two arguments: the header length and the
footer length. It expands into itself, plus the given header and footer. I also
added in a `junk` directive.

```"junk 4
   "a
   "b
   "c
   "d

#2|Quine loops

.p|In the most basic quine loop, as used in Van Mello's generator, we simply
alternate between some set of headers and footers.

```"H1
   "...
   "F1
   "
   "  ==>
   "
   "H2
   "...
   "F2
   "
   "  ==>
   "
   "H3
   "...
   "F3
   "
   "  ==>
   "
   "H1
   "...
   "F1

.p|Let's start with the headers. Here's a simple three level quine loop:

```"COMPILE_MODE
   "compile_print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "repeat 2 12
   "quine 5 0
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "repeat 2 12

.p|The first few lines just print out alternating headers for three levels of
decompression.

```"H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<

```"H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<

.p|Now we have to replenish the next layer in some quineish way. We start by
printing the print lines.

```"H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2

```"H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2

.p|Now we print the actual header data using a repeat block:

```"H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "repeat 2 12

```"H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<

.p|We end it off with a quine to catch up with the rest of our data:

```"COMPILE_MODE
   "compile_print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 2
   "H2
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H3
   "<<<<<
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "H1
   "<<<<<
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "repeat 2 12
   "quine 5 0
   "print 3
   "print 4
   "print 3
   "print 2
   "repeat 2 12

.p|Alternating footers are simple too; we just put every tail into some junk
with a repeat block and copy from it:

```"COMPILE_MODE
   "junk 9
   "repeat 3 6
   ">>>>>
   "F1
   "repeat 3 3
   ">>>>>
   "F2
   "repeat 3 9
   ">>>>>
   "F3
   "compile_print 3
   "repeat 3 6
   ">>>>>
   "F1
